  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\n  We address the non-convex Erdos minimum overlap problem by combining multi-resolution optimization with Bayesian \u00b7 expected improvement (EI) \u00b7 acquisition functions and polynomial chaos expansion (PCE) for efficient surrogate modeling. The coreidea is to:\n  1. Use a multi-resolution strategy to evolve solutions across scales\n  2. Employ PCE to create fast \u00b7 approximate \u00b7 models of the overlap integral\n  3. Use Bayesian optimization with EI to drive the search\n\n  The method avoids local optima by combining coarse-solution \u00b7 guided \u00b7local search and adaptive resolution switching, leveraging dimensionality reduction techniques to manage the high-dimensional search space.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n  Representation: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] defined by N = 2\u00b9\u2070 to 2\u00b9\u00b3 samples\n  Objective: C\u2085(h) = (2.0/N) \u00d7 max_{k} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n  Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n\n  We model C\u2085(h) using PCE:\n    C\u2085_PCE(\u03be) \u2248 \u2211_{\u03b1} \u03b2_\u03b1 \u03a6_\u03b1(\u03be)\n    where \u03be = h scaled to [-1,1]\n\n  Optimization variables:\n    h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with n = N (samples)\n    Projection onto [0,1] is enforced by change-of-variables:\n      \u03be = (2 \u00b7 h/N) - 1  \u21d2 h = (N/2)(\u03be+1)\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n  Phase 1: Parent Validation\n    - If initial_h_values provided, project down to 2\u00b9\u2070 resolution\n    - Ensure sum(h) = \u2248 N/2 \u00b7 dx (dx=2/N)\n\n  Phase 2: Coarse-Scale Initialization (N\u2080=512)\n    \u25aa Use grid-based sampling with periodic symmetry\n    \u25aa Generate 20 candidate solutions:\n        - Interpolations from parent\n        - Random Balanced: half 0.5, half adjusted\n        - Symmetric trimming patterns\n    \u25aa Score candidates using exact overlap integral\n\n  Phase 3: Progressive Resolution\n    \u25aa Increase resolution by powers of 2: 512 \u2192 1024 \u2192 2048\n    \u25aa Refine candidates using:\n        - Functional adaptation (scale preserve)\n        - Coefficient merging\n        - Symmetry block matching\n\n  Phase 4: Bayesian Optimization with VC Dimension Control\n    \u25aa Use PCE surrogate with user-controlled order P (up to ~20)\n    \u25aa Bayesian \u00b7 EI acquisition function selects next trial\n    \u25aa Repair mechanisms:\n        - Integral repair by coordinate penalty\n        - Boundary repair by sqrt mapping\n\n  Phase 5: Local Optimization at Promising Points\n    \u25aa For top 5 candidates at each resolution:\n        - Run stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with:\n            - Step size: 0.05/max(||grad||,5)\n            - Anisotropic move: larger steps for length scales\n    \u25aa Combine improved solutions via interpolation\n\n  Phase 6: Terminal Solution Refinement\n    \u25aa Generate 10 random around final solution\n    \u25aa Score all and pick best\n    \u25aa Final adjustment via polynomial filter\n\n  Search Choices and Variations:\n    - Resolution schedule: N \u2208 {512, 1024, 2048}\n    - Surrogate order: P \u2208 {5,8,12}\n    - Stochastic variation: Gaussian with variance 1.5e-2N\n    - Temperature control: adaptive for phase 3\n\n  Computational Budget:\n    Estimate:\n      - Initial sampling: 10s\n      - Surrogate training (WOPS): 45s\n      - Bayesian optimization: 80% budget\n    Fallback: if any surrogate VCE has negative coefficients\n\n  Validation and Return Contract:\n    Let:\n        h_test = returned solution vector\n        total = np.sum(h_test)\n        n_test = len(h_test)\n\n    Evaluate:\n        assert np.min(h_test) >= 0 and np.max(h_test) <= 1\n        assert abs(total - half) < 1e-2  (where half = n_test*dx)\n        c5_final = compute_c5(h_test, dx=2.0/n_test)\n\n        return h_test, c5_final, n_test\n\n  Special Conventions:\n    - Resolution transfers via resampling\n    - No clipping/hard constraints\n    - Derived c5 using np.correlate(shape='full')",
